LZO is a lossless block compression algorithm focused on fast decompression. It represents a block as a sequence of literal runs and back-references into a sliding dictionary.
The compressor trades ratio for speed: level 1 is a single-pass greedy search, while level 999 performs a lazy search over a large window and is intended for pre-compressed data.
Decompression is deliberately simple and allocation-free. Every opcode is validated so that truncated or corrupted blocks fail with a typed error instead of reading out of bounds.
LZO1X is the variant used by lzop, the Linux kernel, btrfs and SquashFS. LZO1B and LZO1C favour more redundant data, and LZO2A uses a bit-oriented grammar.
